hi i'm mike coleman a developer advocate  here at google cloud  in this video i want to walk you through  how you can use google cloud's migrate  for anthos to move some of your existing  windows workloads from virtual machines  to containers  in just a few steps this video builds on  an earlier one  introducing migrate for anthos if you  haven't checked that video out yet  definitely go do so  as this one assumes you perform the  prerequisites we outlined there  at the end of the last video we had set  up our service accounts enabled the  necessary apis and created a processing  cluster  before we can do the actual migration we  need to complete a couple of windows  specific prerequisites  we need to add a windows node pool to  our processing cluster  and ensure we have a windows build vm we  need the node pool to ensure that we can  analyze windows applications and unlike  with linux applications  there isn't an automated container build  step with windows apps  migrate for anthos will create a docker  file but you'll need a vm on which you  can do the actual docker build  as well as push the resulting image up  to the google container registry  so let's set both of those up i've  logged into the google cloud console and  i'm ready to get started so i'll click  on processing cluster  i'll go ahead and add the node pool  we're going to go with one node  so let's click that there come over here  we're going to change this to the  windows semi-annual channel release  so we'll click make changes i want to  come down here and i want to use an  n1 standard 4 as my machine for the node  pool  and we'll come down and then for  security i want to make sure that i have  full platform access and we'll click  that  and then we'll click create and that  will add the node pool so next thing i  want to do is i want to add  the machine that i'll use to build my  docker images so we're going to come up  over here  and we're going to click on compute  engine virtual machine instances  we'll do create instance  we'll call the machine win build one  we're going to put it in the us  west 2a region we're going to use an n1  standard 4  and we'll use windows server 1909 data  center edition  we want to come down here and ensure we  have full platform scope again  and we'll click create and that will go  off and create our build machine  with those additional prerequisites set  up let's jump into using  migrateforanthos to migrate a windows  application  as a brief recap migrate for anthos has  three primary components  the first is the discovery tool which  assesses how successful a migration of a  given application might be  next is the processing cluster a  kubernetes cluster that is used to  examine the source vm  and create the migration plan as well as  execute the actual migration  and finally mgctl a command line tool  like kubectl for interacting with the  processing cluster  we'll do most of our work in the console  today but i will show you a bit of mig  ctl  and again to quickly recap a migration  generally consists of the following  steps  creating a google cloud project setting  up service accounts and enabling the  appropriate apis  creating a migration processing cluster  and a build vm in the case of windows  choosing and qualifying your workload  defining your migration source creating  a migration plan  and adjusting the migration plan to  ensure it meets your needs  creating the deployment artifacts and  deploying the newly containerized  application  we now have covered the first two items  so let's jump into how you can choose  the right application  the first thing to consider is which  operating systems iis  and net versions can be migrated we  migrate  microsoft windows server 2008 r2 or  higher  microsoft iii 7 or higher web  applications  and asp.net and net framework versions  3.5 or higher  it's important to note that after  migration the resulting container will  run windows server version 1909  in addition to specific versions of the  operating system or app stack  the following application  characteristics make them unsuitable for  migration  applications with dependencies on gpus  or tpus  applications with low level networking  requirements  desktop rdp and vdi applications  and applications with bring your own  licensing in order to help determine if  an application is a good fit  we have the application discovery tool  the tool  outputs a zip file that indicates  whether or not the windows vm is a  potential fit for migration  the name of the file indicates the  results the zip file includes multiple  files with information  on the operating system settings iis  odbc config and the global assembly  cache  i'm not going to go into the zip file  contents in depth and would encourage  you to check out the docs if you need  more information  let's take a look at the migration tool  in action before we do that i want to  give you an overview of the application  we'll be migrating  this is a simple asp.net application  it's got this web front end running on  iis that connects back to a cloud sql  instance  so if i click on courses you can see a  bunch of courses come up those are being  pulled out of the cloud sql database  the virtual machine is right here it's  called symbol university and that's what  we're going to migrate  i've already set up an rdp session into  that box  so we'll move into that now and i've  installed the migrate for anthos  migration tool you can see it right here  collect  info.xc so i'll hit enter and we'll run  that tool it's going to go through it's  going to collect a bunch of system  information this is going to take a  little bit so i'm going to fast forward  the video and i'll rejoin you in a  second  so the tool is finished and if we look  at the file name here  it doesn't say no fit which means we  actually  do have a fit so our application is good  to go and  should be an easy migration we have  completed our prerequisites  and our application 