e a look at it  we're not actually going to edit it i'm  not going to spend a lot of time here  i'll just point out some highlights you  can see here we're running a net four  five classic application  uh here's the web root directory  information like that port 80.  so all of that looks good so let's go  ahead and save that and generate our  artifacts  now this is going to go out and create  our docker file again this is going to  take a couple seconds so i'm going to go  ahead and pause the video  and i'll rejoin you when it's finished  the artifacts have been generated and  those are actually stored in a google  cloud storage bucket  there's a couple different ways you  could get at them you could go to the  bucket directly  or you can actually use mig ctl to give  you the location and then  copy the file down and that's what we're  going to do so i'm going to open up a  cloud shell  and i'm going to issue that mig ctl get  artifacts command and point it at the  migration we just did  and that's going to give me the command  that i need to run so i'm going to copy  that command gsutel cp  gsutil copy and we're going to run that  in our windows  build machine where we're going to build  our docker image so let's run that copy  command  and that's going to bring down the  artifacts.zip file  so it's been copied let's look at the  directory and make sure it's there  clear the screen and we'll do a dir and  there's our artifacts file  so let's expand that archive to get to  the docker file  so expand archive artifacts.zip hit  enter  the archive will get expanded out and we  will have our docker file  all right let's clear the screen let's  take a look at that artifacts directory  to make sure we got our docker file so  do the directory and there's the docker  file so now let's issue our docker build  command  and tag it so it can be pushed up to  google container registry after the  build is finished  so we'll go ahead and get that build  done there it is it's been tagged and  ready to get pushed up to the container  registry so let's do that next  so we'll clear the screen and we'll  issue the docker push command  and that will push it up to google  container registry this is going to take  a few seconds so i'm going to fast  forward the video  and rejoin you when it's done  as that gets pushed up to container  registry the next thing to do  is to actually deploy it to our  production cluster so i've got two  clusters  a processing cluster and a production  cluster and i'm gonna go into cloud  shell and i'm gonna log into my  production cluster  and then to make sure i am on the right  cluster i'm gonna do a get nodes  and make sure that i see all the nodes  of my production cluster which i do  so now let's take a look at our  deployment yaml and you can see nothing  special here  i'm deploying that container registry  image that i pushed up earlier  and so let's go ahead and do that  kubectl apply minus f  symbol deployment.yaml will create my  kubernetes deployment  so give that a minute to settle down and  then we'll check the status here  clear the screen and let's check and see  if our pods are there  and there's our pod check our  deployments  and the deployment is there now let's go  ahead and expose that deployment  on behind a load balancer this will take  a couple seconds i'm going to pause the  video and i'll rejoin you  when it's up and running all right let's  do a coupe ctl get services  and that will list out the ip address of  our load balancer so we're going to grab  that ip address  we're going to come up here into chrome  and open a new tab  paste in that address click there  paste the address in and that will open  the symbol university website  so let's hit enter there's the website  let's click courses we're still  connected to our database  and if i go back into google cloud  platform and i look at the vm instance  i'll see that it's still  stopped meaning that i am in fact  serving my application now  off of the kubernetes cluster out of  that container  so there you have it in this video you  learned about using migrate for anthos  to move windows workloads into  containers  including how to assess your application  create the migration plan  execute the migration plan and deploy  your newly containerized application  be sure to like and subscribe because  we'll be posting subsequent videos in  this series to cover topics such as  best practices for evaluating your  applications how to migrate linux  workloads and more  and to get started on my great for  anthos today visit cloud.google.com  migrate anthos  you

